<main>

<!-- SECTION 1 — HERO -->
<section class="hero-editorial bg-offwhite">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Introducing Compositions</p>
    <h1>Every commitment <span class="accent">has conditions.</span></h1>
    <p class="hero-lede">A commitment does more than state what an organization intends. It creates requirements — and depends on the organization having the conditions to meet them.</p>
    <p class="hero-conditions-row">Governance. Authority. Resources. Incentives. Leadership decisions. Operating practices.</p>
    <p class="hero-lede">When those conditions support the commitment, it has a stronger basis to become believable, adoptable, and sustainable. When they do not, the organization may keep investing in something its own structure is making harder to deliver.</p>
    <p class="hero-lede emphasis">That is the problem Compositions was created to examine.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2 — WHAT COMPOSITIONS IS -->
<section class="bg-white">
  <div class="wrap editorial-inner reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The Advisory Practice</p>
    <h2>Compositions is a structural advisory practice that examines whether organizations are actually arranged to deliver what they say they will.</h2>
    <p>The work begins with commitments organizations are already making and funding — across culture, change, employer brand, leadership, people, and stakeholder relationships. Compositions looks beneath the visible concern to examine the conditions surrounding the commitment: what the organization governs, funds, rewards, authorizes, prioritizes, and reinforces through everyday decisions and operating experience.</p>
    <p>The purpose is not to add another program. It is to make the structural relationship visible enough for leadership to make a better decision.</p>
    <div class="pull-box">
      <p>Compositions provides disciplined structural clarity before organizations spend more trying to solve the wrong problem.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3 — WHY THIS MATTERS -->
<section class="bg-offwhite">
  <div class="wrap editorial-inner reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">When Intention and Conditions Part Ways</p>
    <h2>A commitment can be sincere and still be structurally unsupported.</h2>
    <p>An organization can commit to collaboration while rewarding individual competition. It can commit to wellbeing while depending on chronic overwork. It can hold managers accountable for outcomes they do not have the authority to control. It can launch a transformation while leaving the systems and incentives that sustain the old way of working largely intact.</p>
    <p>None of those contradictions necessarily means leaders were insincere.</p>
    <p class="lead-line"><em>Meaning it was never necessarily the part in question.</em></p>
    <p>The more consequential question is:</p>
    <p class="pull-quote-line">Did the organization create what the commitment actually requires?</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4 — WHAT THE PROBLEM LOOKS LIKE BEFORE ANYONE CALLS IT STRUCTURAL -->
<section class="bg-charcoal">
  <div class="wrap editorial-inner on-dark recognition-head reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow on-dark">What Leaders Often See First</p>
    <h2>Structural problems rarely arrive labeled "structural."</h2>
    <p>They may look like this instead:</p>
    <div class="recognition-list">
      <div class="recognition-item"><p>"People understand the message. They still don't believe it."</p></div>
      <div class="recognition-item"><p>"Adoption was strong at launch. Six months later, we're back where we started."</p></div>
      <div class="recognition-item"><p>"Managers are accountable for this, but they don't control the decisions that determine it."</p></div>
      <div class="recognition-item"><p>"We've invested heavily. We still can't explain why the expected return isn't showing up."</p></div>
      <div class="recognition-item"><p>"We keep relaunching some version of the same thing."</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="recognition-followup">
      <p>Those concerns may lead to another campaign, program, workshop, survey, or relaunch. Sometimes that is the right response. Sometimes the more useful question is upstream:</p>
      <p class="recognition-question">What conditions are producing the outcomes we keep seeing?</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5 — WHAT COMPOSITIONS MAKES VISIBLE -->
<section class="bg-white">
  <div class="wrap editorial-inner reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The Value of the Work</p>
    <h2>Before deciding what to change, see what is actually happening.</h2>
    <p>Compositions examines the relationship between an organizational commitment and the conditions responsible for carrying it. Depending on the question and the available evidence, the work can clarify:</p>
    <div class="value-list">
      <div class="value-item">
        <p class="value-title">Whether the concern is isolated or part of a larger pattern</p>
        <p class="value-body">What appears to be one problem may reflect conditions operating across teams, functions, or commitments.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-item">
        <p class="value-title">Where the organization may be contradicting what it has committed to</p>
        <p class="value-body">The promise, investment, governance, incentives, authority, and operating reality may not be pointing in the same direction.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-item">
        <p class="value-title">What the evidence can — and cannot — responsibly establish</p>
        <p class="value-body">A signal is not a diagnosis. The conclusion should never be stronger than the evidence.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="value-item">
        <p class="value-title">What leadership actually needs to govern</p>
        <p class="value-body">The next decision may not be another intervention. It may be a question of authority, ownership, priorities, resources, incentives, or accountability.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="value-closer">
      <p>The value is structural visibility — seeing the arrangement clearly enough to make a better governance decision.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 6 — WHY COMPOSITIONS SEES IT DIFFERENTLY -->
<section class="bg-offwhite">
  <div class="wrap editorial-inner reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">A Different Unit of Analysis</p>
    <h2>Most organizational problems are managed where they become visible.</h2>
    <p>A communication concern goes to Communications. A culture concern goes to Human Resources. An adoption concern goes to Change. A return concern goes to Finance.</p>
    <p>Those functions may each be doing exactly what they should. But the source of the problem may sit between them.</p>
    <p>The work draws on more than two decades across strategic communications, brand architecture, culture integration, organizational development, leadership, evidence, and the study of how conditions shape patterned outcomes. Across those disciplines, the same thing kept surfacing:</p>
    <p class="lead-line">Problems that look separate can be produced by conditions they share.</p>
    <p>Compositions therefore examines something different: <em>the commitment and the organizational system required to sustain it.</em></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 7 — CLOSE -->
<section class="bg-teal">
  <div class="wrap statement-block reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow on-dark center">The Question Beneath the Commitment</p>
    <p class="governing-line">Every commitment has conditions.</p>
    <p class="bridge">A commitment carries requirements. And it depends on the conditions the organization creates around it.</p>
    <p class="sub-line">So before another promise is made, another initiative is relaunched, or more capital is committed to making something work:</p>
    <p class="question">What does this commitment require of the organization?</p>
    <p class="question">And have we actually created those conditions?</p>
    <p class="final-line">That is where Compositions begins.</p>
